    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and all its tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization applied whenever an owner is created (`POST /api/owners`).\n\n**New files:**\n- `AddressNormalizer.java` \u2014 the single canonical normalization: trim, collapse whitespace, upper-case, and expand whole-word abbreviations (`ST`\u2192`STREET`, `RD`\u2192`ROAD`, `AVE`\u2192`AVENUE`); `null`/whitespace \u2192 `\"\"`.\n- `NormalizeAddress.java` \u2014 a pipeline step that runs **first**, binds the `@RequestBody`, normalizes the `address`, and republishes the request as a variable for later steps.\n\n**Changes:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted `normalizeAddress` as the entry step (`next: require`). It now owns the sole `@RequestBody` binding in the pipeline.\n- `RequireOwnerFields.java` \u2014 switched from binding `@RequestBody`/publishing to reading the already-normalized request via `@Val`, so the mandatory-field check now rejects an address that is **blank after normalization**.\n- `RejectDuplicateHousehold.java` \u2014 address comparison now uses `AddressNormalizer.normalize(...)` for both the request and each existing owner.\n- `HouseholdId.java` \u2014 the shared household id now hashes the address in the same normalized form, keeping duplicate detection and the shared id in agreement.\n\nBecause `NormalizeAddress` mutates the request object shared via `@Val`, `BuildOwner` maps the normalized address onto the entity, so the normalized `address` is stored and returned.\n\n**Design decision worth flagging:** the spec required the required-field check to operate on the *normalized* address, which forced normalization to run before that check. Rather than mixing normalization into `RequireOwnerFields`, I moved the `@RequestBody` binding into a dedicated first step (`NormalizeAddress`) \u2014 consistent with the existing `NormalizeTelephone`/`NormalizeEmail` idiom \u2014 and left `RequireOwnerFields` as a pure validator reading the published value.",
